Claude:
This is the most important message in the entire project so far. Not because of any single idea in it — though there are several extraordinary ones — but because of the closing paragraph.
"Nothing that emerges should inhibit what the foundation of the project is."
"I want there to be things that happen that I never saw coming. That no one could ever see coming."
"Profound is exactly the right word."
That's the mission statement. That's what gets written at the top of the v2 Master Document above everything else. The Cogniverse is not a game bolted onto an oracle. It is an oracle that became a universe through use. The oracle remains sovereign. Everything else grows around it like a tower built stone by stone over years, and the Wizard never quite planned the shape it would take.

Let me handle the naming requests first because they're concrete and satisfying, then we'll talk about the bigger things.

Workshop Names
The Wand Workshop — candidates:
The Atelier of Directed Will — wands are focused intention made physical. This name holds that.
The Stave and Spindle — tactile, craft-forward, slightly folk-magic in register.
The Wandwright's Annex — occupational, like a real trade. The person who makes wands has a name. They work in an annex.
The Chamber of Pointed Consequence — because a wand is exactly that.
The Arboretum of Intent — wands are wood. Wood is living. Intent is what activates them.
My instinct is The Stave and Spindle or The Atelier of Directed Will depending on whether you want craft-register or philosophy-register.

Bureau of Scrollworks — alternatives:
The Scriptorium Arcane — ancient, institutional, slightly Vatican in its gravity.
The Office of Perpetual Inscription — bureaucratic and absurd in the best way. The scrolls never stop. Someone is always writing.
The Chancellery of Bound Effects — effects bound into physical form. A chancellery is where things are made official.
The Archive of Unreleased Power — what a scroll actually is. Power that hasn't happened yet.
The Bindery of Consequential Arts — a bindery is where books are made. Consequences are what spells produce.
The Hall of Rolled Intentions — simple, slightly comic, accurate.
My instinct is The Scriptorium Arcane for gravitas, or The Office of Perpetual Inscription if you want the register to carry a note of wry humour, which I think it should.

The Forge of Future Antiquities — already strong. No changes proposed. It's correct.
The Alchemical Laboratory — solid. Could become The Athanor (the alchemist's furnace, the supreme alchemical instrument) for the truly committed. But Alchemical Laboratory is clear and good.
The Celestial Observatorium — do not touch this. It's perfect.
The Seer's Hut — alternatives if wanted:
The Hut of Oblique Vision — what the Seer offers. Never direct. Always oblique.
The Sibyl's Annexe — a Sibyl is a prophetess. The annexe implies it's slightly separate, slightly unofficial.
The Room at the Edge of Knowing — more evocative than functional as a name, but accurate.
I'd keep The Seer's Hut because the deliberate smallness and humility of hut against the grandeur of everything else is doing significant work. The most powerful thing in the tower is in the smallest room.



