Claude:
Additional Canonical Workshops
Things the tower is still missing:
The Cartographer's Loft — maps the Cogniverse itself. Tracks what has been unlocked, what regions of the system have been explored, what remains uncharted. Not a progress screen. A map, with all the aesthetic weight that implies. Terra incognita at the edges.
The Conservatory — where living things are kept. Familiars. Cultivated materials — certain rare crafting components that must be grown over time rather than found or bought. Patience as a mechanic.
The Observatory of the Self — distinct from the Celestial Observatorium. Where the Celestial Observatorium looks outward, this looks inward. The Aura visualised — not as numbers or bars but as something abstract and aesthetic. A weather system of the self. Available only at high unlock thresholds and even then only occasionally.
The Reliquary — where items of Legendary status are formally enshrined. Not equipped. Retired. Too important to risk. Displayed. Visited. The Tome of Notable Possessions covers active items — the Reliquary is for the ones that have transcended use and become simply significant.
The Printing House — produces the Cogniverse's documents, pamphlets, and announcements. The aesthetic layer of the information economy. When the machine has something to formally declare — a new Entity, a Legendary threshold crossed, a celestial event of significance — the declaration comes from the Printing House, formatted as a broadsheet, typeset as if physically printed.

The Joseph Heller Register
You named it — the tongue-in-cheek bureaucratic absurdist register running through the lore. This deserves to be 
formally enshrined as a tonal principle in the v2 document. The Bureau of Scrollworks, Bindery and Bound Effects is the canonical example.
Every workshop, every emergent event, every info card should be written with this dual register: genuine lore gravity and the awareness that
this is also slightly ridiculous and wonderful. Catch-22 understood that bureaucracy and mythology occupy the same absurd space. The Cogniverse
agrees.


