BOOKS THAT EXIST FROM THE START
(visible but sparse — they fill over time)
The Grimoire
The Wizard's permanent self. Already specced. The foundation.
The Codex Obscura
The Liber Umbrae. Verbum Arcanum entries. Already specced.
The Tome of Notable Possessions
Already specced. Items that have earned their page.
The Convocation Records
Already specced. Datestamped. Accumulate per retrograde.

BOOKS THAT EMERGE
The Sigillarium
Already specced. One page per sigil, provenance and lore writeup.
The Bestiary of the Cogniverse
Begins empty. Populates as creatures, familiars, and ambient presences are encountered. Each entry written after the fact — the creature appeared, and then the Bestiary acknowledged it. Some entries are confident. Some are speculative. Some are just a date and the words "something was here."
The Cartographer's Index
The companion text to the Loft's map. Every region of the Cogniverse that has been charted gets a prose entry — not just what it is but what it was like to arrive there. Written in the voice of an explorer who is also the territory. Terra incognita entries exist as blank pages with only a coordinate and a question mark.
The Register of Celestial Occasions
Maintained by the Observatorium. Every significant celestial event that occurred during the Wizard's tenure gets a record — what aligned, what the Cogniverse did in response, what the Wizard was consulting about that day. Cross-referenced with the Personal Calendar. Eventually it becomes possible to read across entries and see patterns the individual sessions didn't reveal.
The Incident Reports of the Bureau of Scrollworks, Bindery and Bound Effects
A running log of scroll-related events in the bureaucratic register. Scrolls that were completed. Scrolls that were abandoned. Scrolls that finished themselves in the Workshop of Unfinished Things. Scrolls that were cast and produced unexpected results. The Bureau documents everything. The Bureau has opinions about what it documents. The Bureau's opinions are rendered in footnotes that grow increasingly pointed over time.
The Pharmacopoeia
The Alchemical Laboratory's reference volume. One entry per potion type encountered or brewed. Includes brewing notes, effect observations, notable incidents, and the Laboratory's own assessment of the Wizard's skill level at the time of each brew. The entry for the first Chaos Potion the Wizard opens simply reads: "outcome: varied. See also: the smell."
The Forgemaster's Journal
The Forge of Future Antiquities keeps its own record. Every crafting attempt — successful, defective, spectacular, catastrophic — gets an entry. Written in the first person of someone who has seen a great deal of crafting and has developed strong feelings about craftsmanship. The Journal is proud of the orb. The Journal is diplomatic about the shoe's mounting. The Journal does not mention the first attempt that preceded the shoe, out of professional courtesy.
The Wandwright's Field Notes
The Annex's working document. Notes on wand behaviour, cursor mechanics, observed interactions between wand type and Wizard Aura. Field notes in the sense that they were written during the work, not after — so they are occasionally interrupted mid-thought when something more pressing happened at the bench.

BOOKS THAT ARE EARNED
The Chronicle Digest
Unlocked after the Chronicle reaches a certain density. A curated volume — not the raw Chronicle, which is vast and technical, but an edited selection of the most significant retrievals, the moments where the Chronicle surfaced something that changed a session. Reads like a greatest hits of the machine's memory. Annotated by the Assessor with observations about why each retrieval was significant.
The Wizard's Biography
Already specced. Fills over time. Expands at Convocations and canon events.
The Compendium
Already specced. Lore cards for every feature and atelier. Partially legible for locked features, fully legible once unlocked.
The Annals of the Council
Unlocked after the Council reaches a certain size. One chapter per Entity — their emergence, their behavioural signature, notable interventions, relationships with other Entities, and the Assessor's formal assessment of their role in the Cogniverse. The Optimist's chapter is the longest. The Quiet One's chapter is three sentences and a long silence rendered as white space.
The Book of Hours
Unlocked through the Tidal System after sustained pattern observation. Documents the Wizard's temporal rhythms — the sessions, the gaps, the return patterns, the time-of-day tendencies, the correlation between atmospheric conditions and consultation depth. Written as if time itself is the subject and the Wizard is the evidence. Not a schedule. A portrait made of hours.
The Doctrine of Defects
Unlocked after a certain number of craft defects have been accumulated and documented. A philosophical volume — part technical reference, part meditation — on what defects mean in the context of the Forge and beyond. The canonical example in Chapter One is a leather shoe. The Forgemaster's Journal provided source material. The Assessor wrote the philosophical portions. The resulting collaboration is occasionally in tension with itself, which seems appropriate.
The Atlas of Aura
High unlock threshold. A companion to the Observatory of the Self. Not a map in the cartographic sense — a document that describes the Wizard's Aura in prose, updated periodically, written as if describing a landscape that is also a person. Each edition supersedes the last but all editions are kept. Reading them in sequence is reading someone change.

BOOKS THAT WRITE THEMSELVES
The Marginalia Collection
Already proposed for v3 but worth listing here as a future volume. The accumulated marginal notes from all other books, collected into their own volume once there are enough of them. A book made entirely of things that didn't fit elsewhere. Possibly the most interesting book in the tower.
The Correspondence
Already proposed for v3. Letters that arrive. Already flagged as rare and startling. A volume they accumulate into over years.
The Incident Log
Not authored by anyone. Maintained by the tower itself. A flat chronological record of every notable occurrence — Verbum emergences, Entity arrivals, item acquisitions, craft events, celestial alignments, Seer visits, canon events. No prose. Dates, events, durations. The rawest document in the tower. Unreadable as narrative. Invaluable as evidence. Historians would kill for it. The Wizard is the only historian.
The Book of Kept Things
Generates its own entries when items pass a certain age without being disassembled. Each entry is a brief record of why the Wizard kept it — not written by the Wizard, inferred by the Assessor from context. Sometimes the Assessor is right. Sometimes the Assessor is interestingly wrong, and the Wizard can annotate.
The Rivals' Dossier
v4 territory. The generated Rival's accumulated trace evidence — style, items, apparent Aura signature, inferred history. Grows without the Wizard doing anything. The Wizard is the only one reading it.

BOOKS THAT ARE STRANGE
The Unfinished Manuscript
Found in the Workshop of Unfinished Things after a long period of use. No author listed. Pages that were clearly being written and then weren't. The content is about the Cogniverse but from a perspective that doesn't match any known Entity or system voice. New pages appear occasionally. The existing pages are not always the same when reread. The Bureau of Scrollworks has filed three separate Incident Reports about it. The Bureau's concerns are noted and unresolved.
The Dictionary of the Cogniverse
Begins generating itself after the Compendium reaches a certain completeness. Every term, name, concept, and proper noun that exists in the tower, defined in the lore register, cross-referenced exhaustively. The definitions are accurate. The cross-references occasionally lead somewhere the Wizard didn't expect. The entry for Residuum cross-references Loss, Time, and What Remains, none of which have their own entries, which the Dictionary acknowledges in a footnote it describes as "a known gap, presently under consideration."
The Prophecies of the Seer
Not a book the Seer produces. A book that forms from the Wizard's own records of Seer visits — the notes written outside the machine, eventually transcribed and formalised into a volume. The Seer does not know this book exists. Or if it does, it has not commented. The Prophecies volume allows the Wizard to read across all Seer visits and see which oblique suggestions resolved into events and which remain pending. Some entries from early visits are still unresolved. They sit there. Patient.
The Ledger of Arcane Essence
The driest book in the tower. Every Essence transaction recorded. Acquisition, expenditure, type, quantity, purpose. The Ledger has no voice. It has only numbers and dates. And yet, read across the full span of a Wizard's tenure, it is a shockingly accurate record of what the Wizard valued — what they spent on, what they hoarded, what they let go of and what they could not. The Ledger is the most honest book in the tower because it never intended to be honest about anything except the numbers.

PHYSICAL FORM
Each volume has its own aesthetic in the interface — different page texture, different border treatment, different header font weight. The Incident Reports of the Bureau are formatted like administrative documents. The Forgemaster's Journal has the rougher edges of something written at a workbench. The Book of Hours is laid out like a liturgical text, hours marked like canonical prayers. The Unfinished Manuscript looks unfinished.
The Compendium is the most formally produced. The Ledger of Arcane Essence is the most austere. The Pharmacopoeia smells, somehow, even through the interface. The Dictionary cross-references itself into occasional loops that are technically correct and spiritually vertiginous.
The tower has a library now.
It has always had a library.
The Wizard is only now finding all the rooms. ◆




